{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01A9BNs8kv2AwfBagDzEe8Bx","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me analyze the project context carefully to understand the current state and determine what tasks to propose.\n\n## Project Summary\n\nThis is a Go reimplementation of GNU coreutils/moreutils utilities, verified via differential testing. The project uses a specification-driven development pipeline where specs are written first, then code is generated by an AI agent (mage-claude-orchestrator).\n\n## Architecture\n- `cmd/<name>/` - One utility per directory\n- `pkg/testutils/` - Differential testing framework\n- `pkg/sys/` - System calls\n- `pkg/format/` - Output formatting\n\n\n- `pkg/hashutil/` - Hash/checksum shared code\n- `pkg/sizeparse/` - Size parsing\n- `pkg/encutil/` - Encoding utilities\n- `docs/specs/` - SRDs, use cases, test suites\n\n## Current Implementation State\n\nLooking at the `code_status` in the analysis, I need to find the earliest incomplete releases. Release 00.0 covers foundational infrastructure, with magefiles and sys utilities already implemented, but format utilities haven't been started yet.\n\nThe format and testutils use cases are the blockers for completing release 00.0. Moving forward, release 01.1 for cat is the next major gap, while sponge, du, and the trivial utilities are all done. Tee is also complete.\n\nNow I'm looking at the remaining incomplete features across the later releases—head and seq are done in 02.2, wc is finished in 03.0, but nproc is still pending in 03.1. Beyond that, there are several releases with multiple unstarted items: ls in 04.0, logname in 04.1, and echo and tac in 05.0, among others further down the list.\n\nLooking at the requirement_states to identify which SRDs are marked as ready, I see srd004-ts and a range from srd060 through srd114 are all flagged as ready. The SRDs not appearing in this list are either already complete or haven't been tracked yet, so I need to cross-reference which of these ready requirements correspond to the earliest incomplete work items. These are all \"ready\" status. The SRDs for the earlier releases (format = srd003, testutils = srd001, cat = srd005 or similar, nproc = some srd) are NOT in the requirement_states list.\n\nWait, let me reconsider. The requirement_states only contains entries for SRDs that have requirements with status \"ready\". If an SRD is not listed, its requirements might be \"complete\" already. The constraint says: \"only propose tasks for R-items with status 'ready'. R-items with status 'complete', 'complete_with_failures', or 'skip' must not be re-proposed. If all R-items for the current release are complete or skipped, return an empty list.\"\n\nSo for the releases that have use cases marked as \"not started\" in code_status but whose SRDs are NOT in requirement_states, those requirements might have already been completed in a previous task cycle but the code_status hasn't been updated, OR they haven't been tracked yet. Looking at the requirement_states field, it tells me which requirements are ready to be worked on. If a requirement isn't listed, it's either complete or not tracked, and the constraints are clear that I should only propose tasks for R-items with status \"ready\". The SRDs showing as all \"ready\" include srd004-ts for release 15.0, and then a batch from release 08.0 and 08.1 like srd060-date, srd061-sleep, srd062-touch, srd063-timeout, srd064-shuf, srd065-factor, srd066-expr, srd067-split, srd068-csplit, and srd069-join. Continuing through more utility commands across releases 10.0 through 12.0, covering file permissions, user management, process control, and system administration tools. I see the issue now—the requirement_states dictionary is the authoritative source for what I can propose tasks for, so I should ignore SRDs that aren't listed there even if their code_status shows \"not started\". Looking at which releases actually have \"ready\" requirements, I need to find the earliest one and focus my task proposals there. I'm noticing a pattern here—all these incomplete items from earlier releases don't have their SRDs in the requirement_states, but starting from release 08.0, the SRDs actually have ready requirements. Though there are some outliers like srd004-ts in 15.0 and srd058-rm in 07.1 that do have ready requirements, which suggests the recent commits have been filling in some of these gaps.\n\nSince I can only propose tasks for requirements marked as ready, I should focus on the earliest release that has them—that's 08.0 with its suite of utilities like date, sleep, touch, timeout, shuf, factor, and expr. All of these are currently showing as not started in the code status.\n\nThe challenge is that I don't have access to the actual SRD content or the weight information from requirements.yaml, which I'd need to properly estimate task scope and stay within the 4-task limit while respecting weight budgets. The project context provides the high-level structure and requirement states, but not the detailed specifications or metrics required to make informed task proposals. group of 4 requirements forms a logical unit.\n\nPer the constraints, \"Each task must contain at most 4 SRD sub-requirements\". And \"Each task's total requirement weight must not exceed 4.\"\n\nIf each requirement has default weight 1, then 4 requirements × 1 weight = 4, which exactly hits the limit. So each group of 4 requirements (R1.1-R1.4, R2.1-R2.4, etc.) would form one task.\n\nBut srd070-fmt has a different structure with 13 requirement groups, so I'd need to bundle multiple groups together to stay within the 4-requirement limit. That means some tasks would combine R1.1 with R2.1, R3.1, and R4.1, then the next task would take R5.1 through R8.1, and so on.\n\nSince I can't access the requirements.yaml file where weights are stored, I'm working with the assumption that each requirement defaults to weight 1. The key constraint is fitting everything into tasks that don't exceed 4 sub-requirements and 4 total weight.\n\nLooking at the pattern, SRDs like srd060-date with R1.1-R1.4, R2.1-R2.4 structure have 4 requirements per group at weight 1 each, so each group maps cleanly to one task. But SRDs like srd070-fmt with single requirements per group (R1.1, R2.1, R3.1) might be weighted differently—possibly weight 4 each if the total per group is meant to be 4—which would mean each requirement needs its own task.\n\nFor release 08.0, I'm starting with the simpler cases: srd060-date has 16 requirements across 4 groups, and srd061-sleep appears to follow a similar pattern. I'm seeing that each SRD has the same structure—four groups of four requirements—which means I need 28 tasks total across the release, but I can only propose 4 per session. Since release 08.0 is the earliest with ready requirements, I should start there, beginning with srd060-date and considering which utilities are simpler to tackle first. These all build on release 00.0, so that dependency is already satisfied.\n\nLooking at the code status for 00.0's utilities like format and testutils, they're marked as \"not started\" but they're clearly already implemented since other utilities depend on them—the status tracking is probably just outdated. The package_contracts section should clarify what's actually needed.\n\nNow I need to figure out the first four tasks for release 08.0, starting with srd060-date. The challenge is that I only have the requirement IDs (R1.1-R1.4, R2.1-R2.4, etc.) without seeing the actual SRD content, so I'll need to write task descriptions that are general enough for the stitch agent to fill in the details by reading the SRD file directly.\n\nFor each task, I should specify which SRD file to reference, which requirement groups to implement, what files need to be created or modified, and what the acceptance criteria are. Based on the release description, srd060-date implements the date command with configurable output formatting. Following the project's file structure, I'd create cmd/date/main.go for the implementation and cmd/date/date_test.go for differential tests. The constraint limits me to 4 tasks total across 7 utilities, so I need to group them efficiently rather than splitting implementation and tests separately.\n\nGiven that srd060-date has 16 requirements and each task can contain at most 4 R-items, I'm realizing the date utility alone would consume all 4 task slots if I break it down by requirement groups. I need to check whether the weight budget allows this grouping or if I should approach the task breakdown differently.\n\nAssuming default weight of 1 per requirement, four items per group would hit exactly the 4-point limit. The pattern across these SRDs suggests most grouped requirements are weight 1, while single-item groups like those in srd070-fmt are likely weight 4 each. So I'll move forward with proposing 4 tasks for the date utility, starting with the task descriptions and required reading sections. I'm mapping out the task structure where each SRD requirement group gets its own task—Task 0 handles R1.1-R1.4 with the initial main.go creation, then Tasks 1-3 progressively implement R2.1-R2.4, R3.1-R3.4, and R4.1-R4.4 through modifications to the same file. I'm realizing that cmd/date is a main package with no exports, so a contract task isn't necessary—the SRD itself serves as the contract. The pattern from recent commits confirms that each group of four requirements becomes a single task, with the first task using \"create\" for new files and subsequent tasks modifying them. Looking at the commit title pattern, I can see the structure for srd060-date tasks should follow the same format with four separate task groups covering R1 through R4, each containing four requirements. Now I'm working through the weight budget to ensure each task group stays within limits.\n\nFor the task descriptions themselves, I need to structure them as self-contained YAML with the deliverable type, required reading materials, the files to create or modify, mapped requirements from the SRD, design decisions based on the architecture, and clear acceptance criteria that can be verified.\n\nThe dependencies form a linear chain where the first task creates the main.go file and each subsequent task builds on the previous modifications. I'm planning to write out the full task descriptions starting with task 0, referencing the specific SRD requirement IDs and letting the stitch agent pull in the actual content.\n\nFor the first task, I need to specify the required reading materials including the SRD file itself, the SIGPIPE handler pattern from the codebase, and project conventions. The task creates the initial cmd/date/main.go file covering requirements R1.1 through R1.4, with design decisions around SIGPIPE handler installation, exit code handling, and the build output location. The acceptance criteria verify compilation and that the basic structure is in place.\n\nNow looking at the next part—I'm realizing the requirements section is too generic. The stitch agent will have access to the full SRD file, so I should be more specific about what each requirement group actually covers. For the date utility, R1 likely handles core date display functionality, R2 covers format strings, R3 deals with flags and options, and R4 addresses error handling and edge cases. This gives the agent clearer context about what needs implementing. Since I don't have access to the actual SRD, I'll reference the requirement IDs and let the stitch agent handle reading the full specifications from the SRD document itself. Looking at recent commits, they follow a pattern like \"cmd/rm: srd058 R4.1-R4.4\" which tells me exactly which sub-requirements to target.\n\nFor the first task creating a new file, I'll use the \"create\" action, then switch to \"modify\" for subsequent tasks since the file will already exist. I'm also thinking about whether to include tests—the constraint says to split implementation and tests into separate tasks, so with only four tasks available, I should focus these on the date implementation itself and handle tests separately. I'm settling on letting the SRD structure dictate the task breakdown rather than forcing a predetermined pattern—if implementation and tests are grouped together in the requirements, they belong in the same task. Now I need to figure out what reference binary would be appropriate for the date command.\n\nWriting task structure... I'm verifying that all the use cases I'm proposing fall within the allowed release scope, which includes releases from 00.0 through 15.0, and I need to make sure I'm not including anything outside those bounds before finalizing the output.\n\nLooking at the git history, I can see a clear pattern where each task updates the requirement states after completion, moving requirements from ready to in_progress status. Release 08.0 appears to be the earliest one with ready requirements that I should focus on, so I'll proceed with generating the complete output based on that.\n\nFor the date utility task, I need to ensure the stitch agent has access to the SRD as the primary input, plus any existing cmd/date/main.go if this is a follow-up task. The design should follow standard patterns like SIGPIPE handling, using gdate as the reference binary, and LC_ALL=C for consistent test behavior.\n\nLooking at the dependency chain, the date utility will need testutils for differential testing and sys for the SIGPIPE handler, but likely won't require format, hashutil, sizeparse, or encutil. Now I'm ready to structure the final output with proper YAML formatting.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":62762,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":22,"service_tier":"s